1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:10,538
Here is a wire that brings one bit in each clock period.

2
00:00:10,538 --> 00:00:14,782
We want a circuit that watches it and raises its output for one

3
00:00:14,782 --> 00:00:18,354
period whenever it has just seen three ones in a row.

4
00:00:18,454 --> 00:00:22,228
After that it starts counting again from nothing.

5
00:00:22,683 --> 00:00:25,047
Let's see what that means on a stream.

6
00:00:25,047 --> 00:00:29,695
A single one, and then two ones, give us nothing.

7
00:00:29,795 --> 00:00:34,923
Then come three ones, and on the third the output goes high.

8
00:00:35,383 --> 00:00:42,465
With four ones in a row we still get a single pulse, on the third, and the fourth is a fresh start.

9
00:00:42,565 --> 00:00:45,993
Six in a row give two pulses.

10
00:00:47,216 --> 00:00:49,736
Can logic gates alone do this?

11
00:00:49,736 --> 00:00:51,748
Look at these two moments.

12
00:00:51,748 --> 00:00:59,468
In both the input is a one, but at the first the output must be zero and at the second it must be one.

13
00:00:59,568 --> 00:01:06,012
Gates without memory give the same output for the same input, so the answer is no.

14
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:13,280
The circuit has to remember something about the past, but not the whole history.

15
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:17,568
Ask what it must know in order to deal with the next bit.

16
00:01:17,668 --> 00:01:23,668
All it needs is how many ones in a row it has just seen, and that is none, one or two.

17
00:01:23,668 --> 00:01:27,520
On the third it fires and goes back to none.

18
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:31,648
Give those three situations names.

19
00:01:31,648 --> 00:01:39,320
S zero means no ones yet, S one means one so far, and S two means two so far.

20
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:42,734
We draw each as a circle and call it a state.

21
00:01:42,734 --> 00:01:47,036
At any moment the machine is in exactly one of them.

22
00:01:47,483 --> 00:01:50,741
Now go through them and ask what each bit does.

23
00:01:50,741 --> 00:01:59,037
In S zero a one arrives, which makes one in a row, so we move to S one and the output stays zero.

24
00:01:59,137 --> 00:02:04,695
We draw that as an arrow, labelled with the input, a slash, and the output.

25
00:02:04,695 --> 00:02:08,084
If a zero arrives instead we stay where we are,

26
00:02:08,084 --> 00:02:13,203
and that is an arrow from the state back to itself, called a self loop.

27
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:19,200
In S one, another one takes us to S two, still with output zero.

28
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:24,064
A zero breaks the run, so we go back to S zero.

29
00:02:24,516 --> 00:02:27,920
In S two, a one is the third in a row.

30
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:35,016
This is the arrow that carries an output of one, and it leads back to S zero to start over.

31
00:02:35,116 --> 00:02:41,492
A zero from S two also leads back to S zero, with output zero.

32
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:48,888
That makes six arrows, two out of every state, so the machine always knows what to do.

33
00:02:48,988 --> 00:02:52,458
A picture like this is called a finite state machine.

34
00:02:52,458 --> 00:02:56,058
It has inputs, outputs, a fixed set of states,

35
00:02:56,058 --> 00:03:01,772
and arrows that say where each input leads and what to output on the way.

36
00:03:02,216 --> 00:03:04,338
Let's run the stream through it.

37
00:03:04,338 --> 00:03:06,480
A zero, and we stay.

38
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:11,746
A one takes us to S one, and the next zero sends us back.

39
00:03:11,846 --> 00:03:17,774
Then one and one bring us to S two, but a zero sends us home again.

40
00:03:18,216 --> 00:03:21,312
Now one, one, and one more.

41
00:03:21,312 --> 00:03:28,570
On that last arrow the output is one, and we are back in S zero, ready for the next run.

42
00:03:37,016 --> 00:03:40,466
To build this, the states have to become bits.

43
00:03:40,466 --> 00:03:50,358
Three states fit in two bits, so let S zero be zero zero, S one be zero one, and S two be one zero.

44
00:03:50,458 --> 00:03:53,818
Then every arrow becomes a row of a table.

45
00:03:53,818 --> 00:04:00,488
On the left go the present state and the input, and on the right the next state and the output.

46
00:04:00,950 --> 00:04:03,892
Take the arrow from S one on a one.

47
00:04:03,892 --> 00:04:11,872
Its row reads present state zero one, input one, next state one zero, output zero.

48
00:04:11,972 --> 00:04:14,414
Six arrows give six rows.

49
00:04:14,414 --> 00:04:19,960
The pattern one one never appears, because no arrow leads to it.

50
00:04:21,183 --> 00:04:23,205
Look at what this table is.

51
00:04:23,205 --> 00:04:30,813
Its left side decides its right side with no memory involved, so it is a job for plain logic gates.

52
00:04:30,913 --> 00:04:35,435
The only thing that has to be remembered is the present state,

53
00:04:35,435 --> 00:04:39,155
which is two bits, and we know what remembers bits.

54
00:04:39,616 --> 00:04:43,816
So the circuit is a register for two bits and a block of logic.

55
00:04:43,816 --> 00:04:47,546
The register's output is the present state.

56
00:04:47,646 --> 00:04:52,980
The logic takes that and the input bit and produces the output and the next state.

57
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:58,942
And the next state goes around to the register's input, where it waits for the clock.

58
00:04:59,383 --> 00:05:06,767
At each rising edge the next state becomes the present state, and the logic starts on the following bit.

59
00:05:06,867 --> 00:05:12,413
This is the same loop as our running sum, with the adder swapped for a table.

60
00:05:13,650 --> 00:05:16,088
What is inside the logic block?

61
00:05:16,088 --> 00:05:18,110
We can read it off the table.

62
00:05:18,110 --> 00:05:25,488
The output is one in a single row, where the present state is one zero and the input is one.

63
00:05:25,588 --> 00:05:31,696
So the output is the high state bit, and not the low state bit, and the input.

64
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:34,652
The next state works the same way.

65
00:05:34,652 --> 00:05:40,028
Its high bit is one only in the row with state zero one and input one,

66
00:05:40,028 --> 00:05:45,174
and its low bit only in the row with state zero zero and input one.

67
00:05:45,274 --> 00:05:50,858
That is three AND gates and a few inverters, and the machine is complete.

68
00:05:52,083 --> 00:05:55,159
The same recipe works for any diagram.

69
00:05:55,159 --> 00:05:57,923
Here is a smaller one with two states.

70
00:05:57,923 --> 00:06:01,631
On a zero it stays where it is and outputs zero,

71
00:06:01,631 --> 00:06:06,267
and on a one it switches to the other state and outputs one.

72
00:06:06,367 --> 00:06:11,861
One flip-flop holds the state, and a little logic does the rest.

73
00:06:12,316 --> 00:06:15,546
Real processors are full of machines like this.

74
00:06:15,546 --> 00:06:21,770
The controller of a cache, for instance, steps through states as it serves a request,

75
00:06:21,770 --> 00:06:25,870
first checking, then fetching from memory, then storing.

76
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:33,104
Any step by step procedure with a fixed number of situations can be built from a register and logic.

77
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:38,860
So a register in a loop can remember anything we can number,

78
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:42,318
and the logic decides how that memory changes.

79
00:06:42,418 --> 00:06:47,414
There is one more use for a register, and it has nothing to do with remembering.

80
00:06:47,414 --> 00:06:51,518
Sometimes we add one just to make the clock faster.

